# Build trees and fetched data. Corpora, papers, audio and page renders run to about
# 1.9 GB and every one of them is reproducible from the tools in this repository.
# None of it is carried in git.
build/
build-*/

# The rendered site. mkdocs build writes it and the GitHub Action rebuilds it on every push.
# A copy in git is one that goes stale the moment a page changes.
site/

# Cloned dependencies. MMgr used to sit here as four separate partial copies of itself, each one
# byte identical to the original and none of them recording which commit it came from. A stale
# copy and a current one looked the same. `python maint/deps/get_deps.py` clones it instead, and
# git in the clone answers what is checked out. Same rule as build/ and site/: what a tool can
# fetch is not carried here.
#
# ANCHORED, and the slash is the whole of the repair. Written as `deps/` with no leading slash this
# matched a directory named deps at ANY depth, besides the clone directory it was written for it
# also swallowed maint/deps/, where get_deps.py and vendor_test_vectors.py live. Both tools were
# therefore in no commit that has ever existed, while sitting on one machine's disk, and five
# documents instructed a reader to run a file their clone could not contain. Being ignored AND
# untracked, `git clean -xfd` would have deleted them with nothing to restore from.
#
# The rule written to stop unrecorded copies had silently stopped the tool that does the recording
# from being carried at all. A pattern anchored to the repository root says what it meant to say.
/deps/

# The hand extractions and the papers they were read from. Every row is a form transcribed out of a
# published paper. The tables are those papers' text and not this work's to redistribute, and the
# papers are their authors' copyright. Both live in a closed repository, inventoried by
# tools/maintain/corpus_manifest.py and signed there, and reach this tree under build/oracles and
# build/papers through tools/maintain/private_sync.py. The derivations built on them are this work's
# and stay in the repository: the ledger, the bound, the checks and the code are all here.
*.oracle.tsv

# Python caches. The tools here are run directly and cache next to themselves.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# What a LaTeX run leaves beside the source when it is not given an output directory.
#
# maint/texbuild/build_theory.sh compiles with -output-directory and leaves none of this. TeXworks
# and TeXShop compile IN PLACE. A book opened in one of those to look at it comes back with a
# .pdf, a .log, a .toc, a .aux per included chapter and a synctex index. theory/anchor_sift picked
# up twelve such files that way, 705,688 bytes of it one synctex, and the arXiv packager copied
# every one of them into a submission before it learned to skip them.
#
# main.pdf is named instead of *.pdf on purpose. The corpus derivation chapter includes a
# matplotlib figure that is a PDF and belongs to the source.
*.aux
*.log
*.toc
*.lof
*.lot
*.out
*.synctex
*.synctex.gz
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
theory/*/main.pdf
theory/*/*/main.pdf
theory_bucket/*/main.pdf
theory_bucket/*/*/main.pdf

# What bench_raster drops where it is run from. A run writes 22 of these, and the renderer writes
# them beside the working directory instead of under build/, and the repository root collects them
# whenever a bench is run from there. Checked before adding: no tracked file matches any of the
# three. These ignore generated output and no other file.
#
# These are extensions and are deliberately unanchored, unlike `deps/`, which had to become
# `/deps/` after the bare form matched `maint/deps/` at depth and hid two files that were then in
# no commit in this repository's history.
*.pgm
*.raw
*.raw.txt

# Editor and OS leavings.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
